(5682682, 824868, 97941, '<td valign="top">Congrats on the win - I gave it a 10. Figuring out how the title fits in with the image sometimes is part of the fun so don''t be sorry. I didn''t get it until I read your description then I just chuckled. Again, great image and congratulations.</td>', 'Congrats on the win - I gave it a 10. Figuring out how the title fits in with the image sometimes is part of the fun so don''t be sorry. I didn''t get it until I read your description then I just chuckled. Again, great image and congratulations.', '2009-10-10 14:58:04.000000', NULL, 0),
(5682708, 823134, 2764, '<td valign="top">Could this have suffered from "poor voter monitor calibration syndrome"?  I''m looking at it on a very crappy monitor at work and it is much darker than what I saw at home.  To many voters, too dark = oops you made a mistake.  And they go no further.</td>', 'Could this have suffered from "poor voter monitor calibration syndrome"?  I''m looking at it on a very crappy monitor at work and it is much darker than what I saw at home.  To many voters, too dark = oops you made a mistake.  And they go no further.', '2009-10-10 15:53:39.000000', NULL, 0),
(5682724, 823249, 47470, '<td valign="top">This is beautiful.  I''m going to have to disagree with Andrewt and say that the reason why this is so perfect is *because* it''s not oversaturated!  The partial desat makes this image, in my opinion.  It says "autumn" mush more than a colorful image would!</td>', 'This is beautiful.  I''m going to have to disagree with Andrewt and say that the reason why this is so perfect is *because* it''s not oversaturated!  The partial desat makes this image, in my opinion.  It says "autumn" mush more than a colorful image would!', '2009-10-10 16:28:53.000000', NULL, 0),
(5682750, 823792, 9668, '<td valign="top">Awesome shot! Congrats on the yellow.<br/> <br/><table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by LevT:</b></div><hr/><i>Basically, half the time was wasted, and I missed a few good bolts because of that. Then I realized that if the previous shot came in empty (no lightning), I can avoid wasting valuable time by turning the camera off and then immediately back on - this interrupts the noise reduction routine. Maybe this tip can help someone in similar circumstances... </i></td></tr></table> <br/>...Or you could get a 5d Mark II where you can shoot the next picture while the previous image is still processing. =)</td>', 'Awesome shot! Congrats on the yellow. Originally posted by LevT:Basically, half the time was wasted, and I missed a few good bolts because of that. Then I realized that if the previous shot came in empty (no lightning), I can avoid wasting valuable time by turning the camera off and then immediately back on - this interrupts the noise reduction routine. Maybe this tip can help someone in similar circumstances...  ...Or you could get a 5d Mark II where you can shoot the next picture while the previous image is still processing. =)', '2009-10-10 16:59:43.000000', NULL, 0),
(5682770, 823792, 30214, '<td valign="top"><table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by Zoomdak:</b></div><hr/><i> Awesome shot! Congrats on the yellow.<br/> <br/><table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by LevT:</b></div><hr/><i>Basically, half the time was wasted, and I missed a few good bolts because of that. Then I realized that if the previous shot came in empty (no lightning), I can avoid wasting valuable time by turning the camera off and then immediately back on - this interrupts the noise reduction routine. Maybe this tip can help someone in similar circumstances... </i></td></tr></table> <br/>...Or you could get a 5d Mark II where you can shoot the next picture while the previous image is still processing. =) </i></td></tr></table> <br/> <br/>I don''t have a Canon, but I cannot imagine how this might work - because the NR in Nikons is spent not on image processing, but on recording an image with exactly the same settings as the original shot (same ISO and exposure), but with a closed shutter and then subtracting it from the original image. So while the camera is acquiring a 15-sec noise-only image it cannot take another shot.</td>', 'Originally posted by Zoomdak: Awesome shot! Congrats on the yellow. Originally posted by LevT:Basically, half the time was wasted, and I missed a few good bolts because of that. Then I realized that if the previous shot came in empty (no lightning), I can avoid wasting valuable time by turning the camera off and then immediately back on - this interrupts the noise reduction routine. Maybe this tip can help someone in similar circumstances...  ...Or you could get a 5d Mark II where you can shoot the next picture while the previous image is still processing. =)   I don''t have a Canon, but I cannot imagine how this might work - because the NR in Nikons is spent not on image processing, but on recording an image with exactly the same settings as the original shot (same ISO and exposure), but with a closed shutter and then subtracting it from the original image. So while the camera is acquiring a 15-sec noise-only image it cannot take another shot.', '2009-10-10 17:15:32.000000', NULL, 0),
(5682803, 823792, 9668, '<td valign="top"><table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by LevT:</b></div><hr/><i> <table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by Zoomdak:</b></div><hr/><i> Awesome shot! Congrats on the yellow.<br/> <br/><table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by LevT:</b></div><hr/><i>Basically, half the time was wasted, and I missed a few good bolts because of that. Then I realized that if the previous shot came in empty (no lightning), I can avoid wasting valuable time by turning the camera off and then immediately back on - this interrupts the noise reduction routine. Maybe this tip can help someone in similar circumstances... </i></td></tr></table> <br/>...Or you could get a 5d Mark II where you can shoot the next picture while the previous image is still processing. =) </i></td></tr></table> <br/> <br/>I don''t have a Canon, but I cannot imagine how this might work - because the NR in Nikons is spent not on image processing, but on recording an image with exactly the same settings as the original shot (same ISO and exposure), but with a closed shutter and then subtracting it from the original image. So while the camera is acquiring a 15-sec noise-only image it cannot take another shot. </i></td></tr></table> <br/>That makes sense, yeah. I''m not sure how it works either, but I know that I''ve been able to shoot another image while the previous one is noise processing (but also, then it takes that extra time processing after both images, or however many images are shot).</td>', 'Originally posted by LevT: Originally posted by Zoomdak: Awesome shot! Congrats on the yellow. Originally posted by LevT:Basically, half the time was wasted, and I missed a few good bolts because of that. Then I realized that if the previous shot came in empty (no lightning), I can avoid wasting valuable time by turning the camera off and then immediately back on - this interrupts the noise reduction routine. Maybe this tip can help someone in similar circumstances...  ...Or you could get a 5d Mark II where you can shoot the next picture while the previous image is still processing. =)   I don''t have a Canon, but I cannot imagine how this might work - because the NR in Nikons is spent not on image processing, but on recording an image with exactly the same settings as the original shot (same ISO and exposure), but with a closed shutter and then subtracting it from the original image. So while the camera is acquiring a 15-sec noise-only image it cannot take another shot.  That makes sense, yeah. I''m not sure how it works either, but I know that I''ve been able to shoot another image while the previous one is noise processing (but also, then it takes that extra time processing after both images, or however many images are shot).', '2009-10-10 17:41:06.000000', NULL, 0),
(5682835, 822875, 83754, '<td valign="top">Thank you for the comments.  I debated using this shot, or another taken about an hour earlier, before sunset, but I just liked this one better for the darker atmosphere.  The dark clouds to the left are the leading edge a storm coming across the canyon from the north rim.<br/><br/><i>Message edited by author 2010-05-16 09:20:06.</i></td>', 'Thank you for the comments.  I debated using this shot, or another taken about an hour earlier, before sunset, but I just liked this one better for the darker atmosphere.  The dark clouds to the left are the leading edge a storm coming across the canyon from the north rim.', '2009-10-10 18:31:04.000000', '2010-05-16 10:20:06.000000', 0),
